      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The smallest multiple of $11$ in the tens place is obtained by evaluating $11\\cdot 9 = 99$. Then, the result of this is one more than a multiple of $3$ since it is not already divisible by $3$ and has only one factor of $3$. Next, since the number ends in 9, it must be divisible by 9. Then, multiply $99$ by $2 = 198$ in order to obtain the smallest four-digit number that is divisible by $33$. $\\blacksquare$ The smallest multiple of $11$ in the tens place is obtained by evaluating $11\\cdot 9 = 99$. Then, the result of this is one more than a multiple of $3$ since it is not already divisible by $3$ and has only one factor of $3$. Next, since the number ends in 9, it must be divisible by 9. Then, multiply $99$ by $2 = 198$ in order to obtain the smallest four-digit number that is divisible by $33$.\n\nIn decimal, $198$ is the answer.",
